{% comment %}
  The model's inputs, step by step: one simple figure per step (tools/hatteras_model_steps.py, the 1996-2015 run),
  with Back / Next buttons and numbered dots (assets/hatteras/js/model-steps.js). Without the script every step shows.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign ms = '/assets/hatteras/img/model_steps/' | relative_url %}
<div class="hat-steps" id="model-steps">
  <div class="hat-steps-nav" hidden>
    <button type="button" class="hat-steps-prev" aria-label="Previous step">&larr; Back</button>
    <div class="hat-steps-dots" role="tablist" aria-label="Steps"></div>
    <button type="button" class="hat-steps-next" aria-label="Next step">Next &rarr;</button>
  </div>

  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 1">
    <h3><span>Step 1 of 6</span> From photo to model grid</h3>
    <p>Planes with lasers (lidar) measure the height of the ground every metre. The model uses one height for every 10 m square. Here is one 500 m stretch of the island between Avon and Salvo, from the sound on the left to the ocean on the right, three ways.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step1.png" alt="The same 500 m stretch of island three times: an aerial photo, a detailed height map from lidar showing two dune ridges near the ocean, and the coarser 10 m grid the model uses" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 2">
    <h3><span>Step 2 of 6</span> The whole island</h3>
    <p>The model island is 90 stretches like that one, side by side, from Cape Point to just north of Rodanthe. Storms move sand across each stretch; waves move sand along the shore from one stretch to the next. The map is stretched across so all 45 km fit; the island is really 1 to 2 km wide.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step2.png" alt="Map of the height of Hatteras Island from Cape Point to north of Rodanthe at the start of the 1996 run, coloured from low green to high brown and white dunes" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 3">
    <h3><span>Step 3 of 6</span> The storms that happened</h3>
    <p>Every storm in the record from 1996 to 2015 is sent over the island in the year it happened: 207 of them, from Hurricane Isabel to small nor'easters. Where the water and waves reach higher than the dunes, sand washes over onto the island.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step3.png" alt="Chart of 207 storms from 1996 to 2015 by how high the water reached, with Isabel highest at about 5 m and Earl, Irene and Sandy near 4 m" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 4">
    <h3><span>Step 4 of 6</span> Waves and rising seas</h3>
    <p>The sea rises 4 mm a year in the run, close to the 3.8 mm a year measured at the Duck tide gauge over the same years. Waves arrive from the north-east and the south-east, a little more often from the north-east, and move sand along the shore.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step4.png" alt="Left: monthly sea level at Duck from 1996 to 2015 with a rising trend line. Right: a sketch of the island with a thicker arrow for waves from the north-east and a thinner one from the south-east" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 5">
    <h3><span>Step 5 of 6</span> What people did</h3>
    <p>The model follows the management that really happened. In front of NC-12, dunes are rebuilt after storms and sand is pushed off the road; in the villages, part of the overwash is cleared from streets and yards. The road was moved inland north of Buxton in 1999, and sand was pumped onto the beach at the Rodanthe S-curves in 2014.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step5_road.png" alt="Aerial photo of one stretch between Avon and Salvo with the front of the dunes and NC-12 drawn on it, about 170 m apart" loading="lazy">
    <p class="hat-step-note">The model knows how far the road sits behind the dunes on every stretch (above, one stretch; below, the whole island), so it knows where dunes must be rebuilt to protect it.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step5.png" alt="Chart along the island of how far NC-12 sits back from the dunes, with the villages shaded, the 1999 road move north of Buxton and the 2014 nourishment at Rodanthe marked" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <section class="hat-step" aria-label="Step 6">
    <h3><span>Step 6 of 6</span> What we compare with</h3>
    <p>When the run ends, the model's shoreline is compared with what satellites measured: how fast the shoreline moved on each stretch from 1996 to 2015. The closer the two match, the more we trust the model to explore what might happen next.</p>
    <img src="{{ ms }}step6.png" alt="Bar chart along the island of measured shoreline change from 1996 to 2015: blue bars growing near Cape Point and north of Avon, red bars eroding at Buxton, south of Avon and Rodanthe" loading="lazy">
  </section>
  <p class="hat-caption hat-numbered">The inputs of the 1996–2015 run, step by step, drawn from the same files the model reads. Heights are metres above mean high water. Storms: the peak water level at the shore (tide, surge and wave run-up). Satellite shorelines: CoastSat.</p>
</div>
<script defer src="{{ '/assets/hatteras/js/model-steps.js' | relative_url }}"></script>
